I rewrote agv.sh for your simpler workflow: you flash the Nano with the Arduino IDE, and everything on the Pi runs from icons you double-click in the Pi Connect screen share. I tested the download, update, shortcut and command-building logic against a fake repo that copies your folder layout. I could not test the apt install, the camera and Nano checks, the GUI window or the desktop icons on a real Pi, so expect to fix small things on first run.

What’s in your repo right now

Your Iteration 3 folder has only a readme file, so there is nothing to download yet. The script knows this and tells you what to upload.

Step 1: upload to GitHub

Upload these three files to your Autonomou car/codes/Iteration 3 folder (Add file → Upload files):

main_vision.py
agv.sh
nano_motor_safety.ino (kept as a backup; the Pi ignores it)
Step 2: flash the Nano (Arduino IDE on your laptop)
Open nano_motor_safety.ino.
Set Board to Arduino Nano.
Set Processor to ATmega328P (Old Bootloader). Most clone Nanos need this, and if the upload fails, try the other option.
Pick the Port and press Upload.
Open the Serial Monitor at 115200. You should see about 20 lines a second like $TEL,….
Step 3: install on the Pi (one time)

In Pi Connect, use Remote shell, which gives you a normal terminal where pasting works. The Pi needs internet for this step.

bash
curl -fsSLo agv.sh "https://raw.githubusercontent.com/robotahmid07-debug/Techfest-iit-Bombay-International-round/main/Autonomou%20car/codes/Iteration%203/agv.sh"
bash agv.sh install

It installs OpenCV, pyserial, numpy and the other tools, and downloads only your Iteration 3 folder. It then puts four icons on the Pi desktop and creates the agv command. If it asks you to reboot, do so once.

Step 4: daily use (Pi Connect, Screen sharing)

Double-click these icons on the Pi desktop. The first click may ask “Execute?”, so choose Execute.

Icon	What it does
AGV 1 – Check hardware	Tests the Python libraries, the camera (is it reaching 30 fps?), the Nano and sonar, and the display.
AGV 2 – Run + tune	Pulls the newest code, then opens the tuning window in HOLD. Press SPACE to drive, s to save, q to quit.
AGV 3 – RACE	Runs the saved copy with no update and no HOLD, so it drives immediately.
AGV – Update	Downloads the latest code from GitHub.

Your tuning (tuning.json) and logs live in ~/agv-car/data, so an update never overwrites them. If the Pi is offline, it runs the last downloaded copy.

Changing code later
Python: upload the new main_vision.py to the GitHub folder, then double-click Run. It updates itself first.
Nano: change the sketch, upload it with the Arduino IDE, and plug it back into the Pi. Run Check hardware afterwards.
Before the competition: do one final Update while you have internet, then use RACE (or agv run --no-update) so the code can’t change mid-event.
Pi Connect on a Pi with no monitor

Screen sharing needs a running desktop session. On a headless Pi:

Run sudo raspi-config.
Under System → Boot / Auto Login, choose Desktop Autologin.
Under Display → Resolution, set a headless resolution (or plug in a cheap HDMI dummy plug).

If you start the program from the Remote shell terminal instead, it still opens its window on the shared desktop.

If something goes wrong
Symptom	Fix
“main_vision.py is not in your GitHub folder”	Upload it to Iteration 3, then run Update.
“no Nano on USB”	Use a data cable (not charge-only) or try the other USB port.
“Nano does not answer”	Re-upload the sketch, and try the other bootloader option.
Sonar flagged stale	Check Trig on D4, Echo on D2, and 5 V and GND.
“already running”	Close the other window first.

